  <!-- 02 PROBLEM -->
  <div class="section rev" id="problem">
    <div class="eyebrow">02 — Problem</div>
    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <p>Defining the geographic boundary is the first step when creating a hazard analysis in Hazus. Analysts must specify the exact region they want to study, which can range from an entire county to a small census block.</p>
    <p>In the existing system, selecting these boundaries relied heavily on ArcGIS-based hierarchical navigation. Users were required to drill down through multiple geographic levels in a rigid sequence, often mirroring the underlying GIS data structure rather than the analyst's actual workflow.</p>

    <h3>Core problems identified</h3>
    <div class="tri-col">
      <div class="tri-item">
        <div class="tri-num">01</div>
        <div class="tri-title">Unconventional design standards</div>
        <p class="tri-desc">The interface was built around complex GIS design patterns rather than modern web application standards. This created a steep learning curve for new users and made simple tasks unnecessarily difficult.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tri-item">
        <div class="tri-num">02</div>
        <div class="tri-title">Rigid hierarchical navigation</div>
        <p class="tri-desc">Selecting a geographic boundary required drilling down through multiple administrative layers. Analysts who already knew their target location still had to navigate through unnecessary intermediate steps.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tri-item">
        <div class="tri-num">03</div>
        <div class="tri-title">Knowledge barrier for non-GIS users</div>
        <p class="tri-desc">Many intended users — planners and emergency managers — do not have formal GIS training. The existing workflow assumed familiarity with GIS concepts, making it difficult for non-technical users to define their study area.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr/>

