  <!-- 05 KEY DESIGN DECISION -->
  <div class="section rev" id="decision">
    <div class="eyebrow">05 — Key Design Decision</div>
    <h2>Key Design Decision</h2>
    <p>The most significant design challenge was how users select their geographic location. If the user could not easily identify and select the correct jurisdiction, the resulting analysis would be inaccurate or incomplete.</p>

    <h3>The current approach</h3>
    <p>The existing system used a layered, hierarchical model. Users had to navigate downward through a geographic pyramid — state → county → jurisdiction — even when they already knew the exact location they wanted.</p>
    <p>This structure became even more inefficient when users needed to select multiple areas. Selecting several census tracts across neighboring communities required repeating the navigation process from the top of the hierarchy each time.</p>

    <h3>The design exploration</h3>
    <p>Early wireframes explored a refined version of the pyramid model — cleaner, more modern, but structurally the same. Through continued stakeholder discussions, it became clear that the core issue was not the visual design. It was about how analysts actually think about geographic boundaries.</p>

    <div class="baf-diagram">
      <div class="baf-col before">
        <div class="baf-title">Before</div>
        <div class="baf-nodes">
          <div class="baf-node">State</div>
          <div class="baf-connector-v"></div>
          <div class="baf-node">County</div>
          <div class="baf-connector-v"></div>
          <div class="baf-node">Community</div>
          <div class="baf-connector-v"></div>
          <div class="baf-node">Census Tract</div>
          <div class="baf-connector-v"></div>
          <div class="baf-node">Census Block</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="baf-col after">
        <div class="baf-title">After</div>
        <div class="baf-nodes">
          <div class="baf-state">State</div>
          <div class="baf-direct">
            <div class="baf-direct-row"><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">County</span></div>
            <div class="baf-direct-row"><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Community</span></div>
            <div class="baf-direct-row"><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Census Tract</span></div>
            <div class="baf-direct-row"><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Census Block</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <blockquote>
      <p>"When people think about where they live, they don't start from the top of a geographic pyramid. They already know the place they're looking for. I wanted the interactive map to reflect that mental model instead of forcing users through a rigid hierarchy."</p>
      <cite>The Insight</cite>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>The solution</h3>
    <p>Instead of forcing users to follow a strict hierarchical drill-down, the redesigned workflow allows analysts to first select a state and then choose the geographic boundary type they want to work with — county, community, or census block — in a single step.</p>

