  <!-- 06 OUTCOMES -->
  <div class="section rev" id="outcomes">
    <div class="eyebrow">06 — Outcomes &amp; Reflection</div>
    <h2>Outcomes &amp; Reflection</h2>
    <p>The redesign transformed OpenHazus from a tool that required specialist knowledge into an interface accessible to all user groups regardless of GIS background.</p>

    <h3>What was achieved</h3>
    <div class="bi-col">
      <div class="tri-item">
        <div class="tri-num">01</div>
        <div class="tri-title">Modern design standards adopted</div>
        <p class="tri-desc">The tool now aligns with modern web conventions, significantly reducing the onboarding barrier for new users.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tri-item">
        <div class="tri-num">02</div>
        <div class="tri-title">Direct jurisdiction access</div>
        <p class="tri-desc">The legacy pyramid flow is replaced, users can now reach their target location in a single step, eliminating unnecessary navigation for both expert and non-specialist users.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ann">Even one metric here, number of steps reduced, or a short stakeholder quote — would significantly strengthen this section.</div>

    <div class="decision-box" style="margin-top:32px">
      <div class="decision-label">What I learned</div>
      <p class="decision-text">Working within constraints, whether due to limited research or technical challenges, does not require compromising design quality. It requires being more deliberate about the reasoning behind each decision.</p>
      <p class="decision-text" style="margin-top:12px">Collaborating with other UX designers strengthened my ability to quickly align on direction and present a unified perspective to stakeholders, even when internal views differed.</p>
      <p class="decision-text" style="margin-top:12px">

      <div class="decision-label">What I Would do differently</div>
      <p>I would validate the jurisdiction model earlier in the process. Jumping into the design without fully understanding the jurisdiction model made the process much harder. We went through many rounds of trial and error, which caused a lot of delays.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

